WEBVTT

00:02.898 --> 00:05.919
We study low-level dexterous manipulation:

00:06.094 --> 00:10.136
approaching, grasping, and reorienting
diverse objects to specified goal poses

00:10.148 --> 00:11.093
with a generalist policy.

00:13.887 --> 00:17.506
A common approach trains object-centric
reinforcement learning policies in

00:17.518 --> 00:20.919
simulation, rewarding progress toward the
object's goal pose.

00:21.548 --> 00:23.276
But learning from scratch is difficult:

00:23.733 --> 00:25.808
limited exploration can prevent the policy

00:25.820 --> 00:28.297
from discovering stable grasps for
reorientation.

00:28.987 --> 00:33.399
X-Reset makes exploration easier by
resetting the robot to hand-object states

00:33.477 --> 00:34.584
demonstrated by a human.

00:36.942 --> 00:39.321
First, we generate cross-embodiment
resets.

00:40.598 --> 00:44.021
We kinematically retarget human
hand-object poses to the robot.

00:45.087 --> 00:48.684
Because human and robot hands differ,
these states can contain errors,

00:48.881 --> 00:50.576
such as fingers penetrating the object.

00:52.598 --> 00:54.858
We filter out unstable states in
simulation,

00:55.120 --> 00:57.173
retaining stable ones for the reset bank.

00:58.887 --> 01:02.039
Next, we train an LSTM policy with PPO.

01:02.687 --> 01:04.654
Its inputs are current and goal object
keypoints,

01:04.666 --> 01:07.154
together with robot proprioception.

01:07.787 --> 01:11.732
During training, we mix ordinary
environment resets with occasional resets
to

01:11.744 --> 01:13.547
retargeted hand-object states.

01:15.387 --> 01:19.821
Using the same training recipe, X-Reset
substantially improves generalist policy

01:19.833 --> 01:21.865
learning across all three embodiments.

01:27.387 --> 01:31.626
We train on DexYCB and evaluate zero-shot
on held-out EBench objects.

01:33.587 --> 01:37.010
As the human demonstrations cover more
hand-object diversity,

01:37.022 --> 01:39.771
X-Reset generalizes better to unseen
objects.

01:42.387 --> 01:45.963
X-Reset also provides a pretrained
backbone that can be fine-tuned on new

01:45.974 --> 01:48.952
objects, without any new human
demonstrations.

01:51.587 --> 01:54.271
At the same total training budget,
including pretraining,

01:54.511 --> 01:58.228
fine-tuning substantially outperforms
training from scratch on the new objects,

01:58.468 --> 02:00.728
with much lower variation across training
seeds.

02:05.398 --> 02:08.495
Because X-Reset does not require strict
imitation of human trajectories,

02:08.507 --> 02:11.343
even imperfect hand-object poses can

02:11.518 --> 02:13.734
provide useful starting points for
exploration.

02:14.587 --> 02:15.826
Adding noise to the offline

02:15.837 --> 02:18.576
hand-pose estimates degrades performance
only modestly.

02:18.935 --> 02:20.021
Even the highest tested noise

02:20.033 --> 02:23.195
level outperforms training without
hand-object resets.

02:25.398 --> 02:29.865
The learned behaviors also transfer
zero-shot from simulation to the real
robot,

02:29.877 --> 02:31.713
on both seen and unseen objects.

02:38.187 --> 02:42.154
These real-world examples also show
robustness to external perturbations.

02:45.587 --> 02:47.793
After human contact, the policies continue

02:47.805 --> 02:50.717
reorienting the objects toward their goal
orientations.

02:52.459 --> 02:53.154
Thank you for listening!
